 # 2D Gaussian Projection with Covariance Stabilization
 
-Also called: 2D Gaussian Projection, EWA Projection, Elliptical Weighted Average projection, 2D Covariance Projection, Covariance Projection, Screen-space Gaussian Projection, EWA Splatting Projection, Perspective Projection of 3D Gaussians, Perspective-correct covariance projection
+Also called: 2D Gaussian Projection, 2D covariance transformation, EWA Projection, Elliptical Weighted Average projection, Linearized 2D Gaussian Projection
 
 ## Overview
-This method projects a 3D Gaussian primitive, defined by its mean and covariance in world space, onto the 2D image plane to produce a screen-space Gaussian ellipse (splat) for rasterization [S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering anisotropic, perspective-distorted 3D distributions efficiently by approximating the non-linear perspective projection with a local affine transformation, thereby preserving the Gaussian form in 2D [S1, S3, unofficial].
+This method projects a 3D Gaussian primitive, defined by a mean and a covariance matrix in world space, onto the 2D image plane to create a screen-space Gaussian splat. It solves the problem of rendering millions of 3D Gaussians efficiently by avoiding ray marching; instead, it approximates the non-linear perspective projection with a local affine transformation, preserving the Gaussian form as a 2D ellipse [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from naive point-splatting by accounting for anisotropic spread and orientation, and from exact perspective projection by using a first-order Taylor expansion (Jacobian) to maintain computational tractability and differentiability [S3, S5, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-- 3D Gaussian parameters: mean $\mu \in \mathbb{R}^3$ (world space), rotation quaternion $q$, scale vector $s$, opacity $\alpha$, and spherical harmonic coefficients $h$ for color [S6, unofficial].
-- Camera parameters: View matrix $V$, Projection matrix $P$, image width $W$, height $H$, focal lengths $f_x, f_y$, principal point $(c_x, c_y)$ [S1, S6, unofficial].
+- 3D Gaussian parameters: mean $\mu \in \mathbb{R}^3$, rotation quaternion $q$, scale vector $s$, opacity $\alpha$, spherical harmonic coefficients $h$.
